hey everyone it's lucy here in this  video i'm going to be talking about  something i'm super passionate about  which is how you can use linkedin to  find your job in 2021 i have my linkedin  logo here i have my nanoleaf set to blue  so everything is good for the video and  i'm going to be sharing everything i  wish i had known in my first two years  of university because i really believe  that these tips will help anyone in uni  anyone looking for a job in tech and  anyone who's already in tech but looking  towards progressing their career in the  future so let's jump straight into it  alright so before you do anything on  linkedin before you start connecting  with people before you start applying to  jobs what you want to do is you want to  be optimizing your linkedin profile when  you start off on linkedin when you  create an account you have essentially a  blank canvas and it's up to you to fit  it in you fill in your profile photo  your header and also all of your  experiences as well as some background  information about you so there's so much  for you to fill in and you really have  to be strategic about first of all  making sure that you have something for  all of those key fields but also being  intentional about how you phrase all  your experiences so now that you have  your linkedin profile all set up ready  to go what you want to do is look for  jobs and i know linkedin has the classic  job tab  on the side for you to search for all  the jobs out there and it's definitely  something very powerful because the  filtering system is just amazing you can  filter by company you can filter by if  it's a full-time employment part-time  employment what you want to do though is  not just apply directly through linkedin  jobs you want to be strategic about this  because when you look at these jobs a  lot of them have you know over 100  people have applied to this job and if  you just submit an application there  it's very easy for that to be just be  lost in the amount of applications that  already have been submitted  so what you do next which brings me on  to the next tip  is to connect with people in companies  and roles that you're interested in  and let's say for example you're  interested in becoming a solutions  architect at amazon web services you  search up the title solutions architect  manager or you search up recruiter and  then you feel to buy amazon web services  and then what you do is you can start by  following them once you follow them on  linkedin you'll be able to see all the  activity if they comment on a recent job  sharing you'll be able to see that  directly on your feed and that way you  can see the top jobs that they're  recruiting for in the company now you  might be wondering well that's good and  all i know now what top jobs people are  looking for  i mean do i just apply to the job what  do i do next right so next what you do  is you  send the recruiter or hiring manager a  message letting them know that you're  interested in the company you can send  their resume through linkedin as well  through linkedin messages and i would  say typically  you don't really get a reply  half the time you do half the time you  don't just because a lot of other  students are implementing the same  strategies as well but it's definitely  good to put something in so that they  would know that you're interested in the  job and you have applied so last year i  saw on my linkedin feed that a manager  at aws was looking for an intern someone  who was able to work part-time for six  months and at that time it sounded like  a really good opportunity for me to  start my career in the tech industry so  i applied to the role i messaged the  manager asking for a half an hour coffee  chat he didn't end up directly replying  to me but he forwarded my details to the  early careers program manager who set up  a conversation with me and were able to  start the interview process basically  the day after so that was just an  example of how i was able to use  linkedin to land my first internship and  i ended up getting that internship so  that was the internship i ended up doing  last year at aws so hopefully that gave  you a bit of an insight into what  strategy you can use on linkedin to try  and stand out in the application process  but something else you can do alongside  this is to search for other people  working in the company who you can ask  to get a referral from so again you  search solutions architect on the search  bar filtered by the company and then  you'll be able to see all the solutions  architects working at amazon web  services you can send them a  personalized message asking for a  one-on-one chat maybe a half an hour  virtual coffee catcher again maybe half  of them reply half of them will just  ignore your message because we get a lot  of messages on linkedin so for the ones  that do reply set up the coffee chat  with them ask about what they do on a  day-to-day basis and ask for some tips  on how you can land a job at aws you can  send them your resume as well as the job  link that you're interested in and then  asking them for a referral and a lot of  the time most of the time people are  very willing and happy to help you out  by suggesting you for the role because  you know you've had that coffee chat  they know that you're someone who is  interested in the role and someone who  is suitable for the role as well based  on your discussions  and then  they're happy to refer you as well  because there's a referral bonus that  employees typically get if the candidate  successfully joins the company now you  might be asking me well lucy i've  applied to all these jobs and i've used  your strategies already about just  messaging people directly about the job  and also trying to get a referral but  what if we're at a period right now  where there's no jobs out there or at  least no jobs that interest me which i  totally understand because for  internships and grad roles they  typically recruit during a specific  period in the year let's say earlier on  from february to may and right now it's  august right there might not be any job  listings that interest you and there  might not be anything that you want to  really apply for and do as a role what  you can do during this period is to  slowly build up your personal brand and  also stay engaged with other people's  posts so the first one might be a bit  more trickier and you might have to  leave your comfort zone more so let's  start off with the second so the second  one is to stay engaged with other  people's posts so what you do is since  you've already you know connected with  all these people who are in companies  you're interested in keep liking their  posts  leave meaningful comments and if you see  a job that you might not be interested  in and you know a friend who might be  interested tag them below in the  comments and that way you slowly build  up a presence on linkedin because 99 of  people on linkedin don't make posts not  many people like and comment other  people's posts on a regular basis so  even just by doing that it really helps  you stand out okay so the final tip is  to start posting on linkedin and there  are so many reasons why  and there are so many reasons you might  not want to post on linkedin so let's  start off with the benefits of posting  on linkedin because honestly you  probably know most of the benefits  already it reinforces your personal  brand it helps keep a repository of  things that you've done cool things  you've worked with in the past that you  want to share with your network it helps  you get visibility in front of  recruiters hiring managers or just  people in your target industry in  general but then there's a few things  that might be stopping you you might be  scared what other people might think you  might be worried about public judgment  you might also be not really sure what  sort of posts you want to make it but  the reason you're watching this video is  because you want to get a job in tech  right and making linkedin posts is one  of the best ways to go about this so  back in may last year i made my first  ever linkedin post and i was shaking  shivering i swear i had so much anxiety  just thinking about it but the reason i  decided to make that linkedin post was  because i had a mentor who really  encouraged me what he said was as long  as your post does two things so first of  all as long as it adds value to your  linkedin network and secondly it  reinforces your personal brand then that  post is worth posting and that really  helped shift my mindset because before i  was always thinking well what are other  people going to think of me instead of  thinking what value i can bring from all  the experiences i've had in university  if i was to share that on linkedin and  help other people out along the way of  course there are going to be people who  judge you people are going to be  thinking well why is she always on  linkedin doesn't she have a life outside  of linkedin i don't know i think those  are the people who are on the extremes  there might be people who just don't  really care when they see your post  they're like oh it doesn't really apply  to me which is fine but what you want to  be targeting is that 10 or 20 of people  who are following you for your content  and who are actually gaining value from  it for example when i made a post about  six lessons i learned from my first six  months of being an amazon intern i had a  lot of people commenting down below on  how the article has really helped them  with their own internship and a lot of  messages as well from people who are  thanking me for actually creating an  article and looking back i think that  article probably took around three hours  to make but it was really worth it  because it ended up helping out a lot of  people along the way and so i guess the  main point of this is to not be afraid  of starting to post on linkedin and also  realizing that there are so many  benefits that will come out of it  alright so just to wrap up if you were  to remember one thing from this video  it's to start posting on linkedin  because linkedin will help you not just  find a job in tech but also  be something that would help you build  up a personal brand in the long run i  really hope this video was insightful to  some of you who are looking for a job in  tech this is just a lot of things that i  wish i had known in my first couple of  years when i was looking for jobs and if  you have any questions or comments  please leave it down below if you found  this video helpful in any way leave it a  like and subscribe to this channel to  stay updated with more tech career and  student advice bye for now

